  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4t)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is also  $\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
